{{smaller block|Oaths of allegiance were ''tendered'' too lightly by the Neapolitans to carry the same weight as in other nations.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 10.}}}}

2. To offer in payment or satisfaction of some demand or obligation: as, to ''tender'' the (exact) amount of rent due.

{{smaller block|Shall any other pay my debt, while I
Write myself bankrupt? or Calista owe
The least beholdingness for that which she,
On all the bonds of gratitude I have seal'd to,
May challenge from me to be freely ''tender'd?''
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and Massinger''?), Lovers' Progress, v. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|It shall be the duty of the seller, on maturity of the contract (i. e., the last day specified therein), to ''tender'' the goods between the hours of 10 o'clock {{sc|a. m.}} and 3 o'clock {{sc|p. m.}}, whereupon he shall be entitled to payment in full therefor before the last named hour.
{{right|''New York Produce Exchange Report'', 1888–9, p. 264.}}}}

3†. To show; present to view.

{{smaller block|Tender [see ''tender''<sup>1</sup>] yourself more dearly;
Or . . . you'll ''tender'' me a fool.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 3. 109.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To make a tender or offer; especially, to offer to supply certain commodities for a certain period at rates and under conditions specified, or to execute certain work: as, to ''tender'' for the dredging of a harbor.

'''tender<sup>2</sup>''' (ten′dėr), ''n.'' [< ''tender''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.''] 1. An offer for acceptance.

{{smaller block|I send you a Coppy of the Draught to shew to Mr. Vicechanceler, with ''tender'' of my service.
{{right|''H. Spelman'', in Ellis's Lit. Letters, p. 161.}}}}

{{smaller block|With a ''Tender'' of my most humble Service to my noble good Lady.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. v. 17.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
Specifically—2. In ''law'', an offer of money or any other thing in satisfaction of a debt or liability; especially, the production and offer to pay or deliver the very thing requirable by a contract.

{{smaller block|When Lard or Provisions are rejected under final appeal, if tendered on a seller's option, all expenses shall be paid by the seller, and it shall be held that no ''tender'' has been made.
{{right|''New York Produce Exchange Report'', 1888-9, p. 181.}}}}

3. An offer in writing made by one party to another to execute some specified work or to supply certain specified articles at a certain sum or rate, or to purchase something at a specified price.

{{smaller block|The privilege of selling to railway-passengers within the precincts of the terminus is disposed of by ''tender''.
{{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, I. 291.}}}}

{{smaller block|Of the three larger vessels, ''tenders'' were received for the Proteus and Neptune, and, the bid for the latter being the lower, it was accepted.
{{right|''Schley and Soley'', Rescue of Greely, p. 38.}}}}

4. Something tendered or offered.

{{smaller block|That you have ta'en these ''tenders'' for true pay,
Which are not sterling. ''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 3. 106.}}

{{smaller|'''Legal-tender currency''', currency which can lawfully be used in paying a debt. All the gold coins of the United States are a legal tender in all payments at their nominal value, when not below the standard weight and limit of tolerance provided by law for the single piece; and when reduced in weight below such standard tolerance, they are a legal tender at a valuation in proportion to their actual weight. The silver dollar of 412½ grains is a legal tender for all debts and dues, public and private, except when otherwise expressly mentioned in the contract. The silver coins of the United States of smaller}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|except gold-note banks.—'''Plea of tender''', a plea by a defendant that he has made due tender, and has remained always ready to satisfy the plaintiff's claim, and now brings the sum demanded into court.—'''Tender of amends''', an offer by a person who is charged with a wrong or breach of contract to pay a sum of money by way of amends.—'''Tender of issue''', a pleading which in effect invites the adverse party to join issue upon it.}}

'''tender'''<sup>3</sup> (ten′dėr), ''n.'' [< ''tend''<sup>2</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>; partly by apheresis from ''attender''.] 1. One who tends; one who attends to, supervises, or takes care of something; a nurse: as, a machine-''tender''; a bar''tender''.—2. ''Naut.'', a vessel employed to attend a larger one for supplying her with provisions and other stores, or to convey intelligence, orders, etc.

{{smaller block|Here she comes i' faith full Sail, with her Fan spread and Streamers out, and a Shoal of Fools for ''Tenders''.
{{right|''Congreve'', Way of the World, ii. 4.}}}}

3. A boat or ship accompanying fishing- or whaling-vessels; a lighter. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In the menhaden-fishery, a vessel or boat employed to carry the fish to the factories. These tenders have an average capacity of 250 barrels, though they are now often built of a larger size, some carrying 600 barrels. (''b'') A vessel sailing from San Francisco to the Arctic regions, to carry supplies to the whale-ships, and bring back oil and bone, to be sent east by rail.}}

4. In ''rail.'', a carriage attached to the locomotive, for carrying the fuel, water, etc. See cuts under ''passenger-engine'' and ''snow-plow''.

{{smaller block|We supplied the ''tender'' and fire with wood, and, in short, pretty much ran the train as we pleased.
{{right|''The Century'', XL. 622.}}}}

