¶
{{smaller block|Well we know your ''tenderness'' of heart.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., iii. 7. 210.}}}}

{{smaller block|We went to see the stables and fine horses of w<sup>ch</sup> many were here kept at a vast expense, with all the art and ''tendernesse'' imaginable.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, July 22, 1670.}}}}

{{smaller block|Eleven half sheets marbled (like smoke) after a different manner, bit with great curiosity and ''tenderness.''
{{right|''H. Wanley'', in Ellis's Lit. Letters, p. 276.}}}}

{{smaller block|There was great ''tenderness'' over the bowels, especially in the right iliac region.
{{right|''J. M. Carnochan'', Operative Surgery, p. 156.}}}}

'''tender-sided''' (ten′dėr-sī″ded), ''a.'' ''Naut.'', crank, as a vessel; careening too easily under press of sail.

'''tendinal''' (ten′di-na̤l), ''a.'' Same as ''tendinous.'' [Rare.]

{{smaller block|A ''tendinal'' slip is shown cut short, of which he says nothing, but which evidently belongs to this muscle.
{{right|''Science'', IX. 624.}}}}

'''tendineal''' (ten-din′ē̤-a̤l), ''a.'' [< NL. ''tendo'' (''tendin-''), a tendon, + ''-e-al.''] Same as ''tendinous.'' [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Special development of its ''tendineal'' portion aids in strengthening the tensor propatagii.
{{right|''Science'', X. 71.}}}}

'''tendines,''' ''n.'' Plural of ''tendo.''

'''tendinosus''' (ten-di-nō′sus), ''n.''; pl. ''tendinosi'' (-sī). [NL. (sc. ''musculus''): see ''tendinous.''] A muscle of the back of the thigh whose tendon forms one of the inner hamstrings: usually called ''semitendinosus.'' ''Coues'', 1887.
